CYCLE=0
IDEA=$'M2 — Edge routing policy + scoped teammate send_to (the comms substrate)\n\nThis is milestone M2 of the Workflow Shape Composition arc. M0 (shape-as-data +\nshape-gate) and M1.5 (async/resurfaceable shape-gate) already shipped. M2 makes the\ntopology recorded in M0 actually GOVERN message flow instead of being decorative\nmetadata. Scope this as ONE slice covering all five components below.\n\nOUTCOME\nThe Topology recorded at instantiate_shape time (broker.py: Topology.edges + per-edge\nmode) starts enforcing routing. Per-edge mode is honored by the broker; teammates can\nmessage each other but ONLY along their declared out-edges, and ONLY within a\ncircuit-breaker budget that force-inserts the lead before anything runs away.\n\nIN SCOPE (all five — single slice):\n\n1. Per-edge routing enforcement in the broker.\n   Three modes:\n   - gated  = lead inbox first (today\'s behavior; the DEFAULT, resolved 2026-06-11)\n   - tee    = delivered direct to recipient AND cc\'d to the lead\n   - direct = delivered direct to recipient, logged to the broker, NOT in lead inbox\n   Today broker send routes purely by `recipient`. Add the policy gate keyed on the\n   recorded Topology.edges for the active topology. Messages with no governing edge\n   fall back to today\'s behavior (lead-routed / gated).\n\n2. Scoped teammate send_to.\n   Teammates gain a send capability scoped to their declared out-edges ONLY — the\n   topology IS the authorization layer. This is the core structural change. Today\n   `recipient=env.sender` is hardcoded in sdk_teammate.py (~line 1706) and teammates\n   hold no send tools. A send to a non-declared recipient must be rejected (loudly,\n   not silently dropped).\n\n3. Neighbor adjacency injection at spawn.\n   Each teammate is told who it is wired to and how, e.g. "you can consult\n   plan-reviewer (direct); verdicts arrive from slice-reviewer (gated)". Without this\n   the edges exist but never get exercised by the teammates.\n\n4. Circuit breaker on direct/tee edges — NON-NEGOTIABLE autonomous-chatter guard.\n   - max exchanges per edge before the lead is force-inserted\n   - per-edge token/turn budget\n   - deadlock detection (A waits on B waits on A)\n   direct edges DO NOT SHIP without this guard in place.\n\n5. Dashboard observability.\n   - edges animate on the dashboard when messages flow across them\n   - click an edge -> see that edge\'s message log\n   - operator can PROMOTE an edge to `gated` mid-run to step back onto it\n   Honor the multi-instance leader/follower rule (see CLAUDE.md): any NEW per-instance\n   endpoint MUST carry crew_id and proxy leader->follower, and needs a multi-instance\n   test, not just a single-instance one.\n\nNON-NEGOTIABLES (the moat invariants — do not violate):\n- Observable by construction: every cross-teammate message still routes through the\n  broker — sequenced, logged, surfaced to Mission Control. NO off-broker peer channels.\n- Coordinator stays on judgment edges: per-edge policy may remove the lead from RELAY,\n  never from GATE.\n- Default edge mode is `gated`. Loosening an edge to tee/direct is always an explicit,\n  deliberate act — never the default, never by omission.\n- Bonded to the Claude Agent SDK / existing SdkTeammate+broker substrate. Do NOT adopt\n  an external orchestration runtime.\n\nEXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE (later milestones — do not build these here):\n- The adaptation algebra verbs add_node / swap / augment / set_gate / drop (that is M3).\n- The blessed shape library + lead right-sizing classifier (that is M1).\n- Re-authoring RepoReactor as a native heavy-feature shape (that is M4).\n- Any autonomous / memory-driven adaptation or widening of lead latitude (that is M5).\n\nVALIDATION EXPECTATIONS (two layers, happy + sad, per the repo\'s standards):\n- Broker per-mode routing unit tests: gated routes to lead inbox; tee delivers direct\n  AND cc\'s lead; direct delivers direct + logs but stays out of lead inbox.\n- Integration: a `direct` edge ping-pong between two teammates stays OFF the lead\'s\n  inbox but DOES appear in the broker message log + the dashboard state.\n- Sad: exceeding the circuit-breaker budget force-inserts the lead; a scoped send_to to\n  a non-declared recipient is rejected.\n- Dashboard: a multi-instance test (leader aggregating a follower\'s edges), not just a\n  single-instance test — per the CLAUDE.md leader/follower trap.\n- The FULL test suite (uv run pytest) must stay green — M2 changes widely-consumed\n  broker/teammate behavior, so validation must run the whole suite, not a -k subset.\n\nCONTEXT TO READ:\n- doc/design/workflow-shape-composition-feature-draft.md (esp. the M2 section, the five\n  design invariants, the adaptation/algebra deferral, and the touch-points in section 6)\n- doc/design/workflow-shape-composition.md (the thesis — "edges carry a routing policy",\n  "why this passes the acid test", the moat argument)\n- claude_crew/broker.py (Topology state: edges + per-edge mode; the send/route path)\n- claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py (the hardcoded recipient=env.sender around line 1706; this\n  is where scoped send_to + neighbor injection wire in)\n- claude_crew/server.py (the lead MCP tool surface — currently 16 tools)\n- claude_crew/ui_server.py + the dashboard html (edge animation, click-edge->log,\n  promote-edge control; AND the multi-instance leader/follower aggregation rule)\n- claude_crew/shapes.py (Shape/ShapeNode/ShapeEdge schema; edge `mode` field)\n- CLAUDE.md (the "Dashboard is a multi-instance LEADER" section — the load-bearing\n  per-instance-endpoint rule)'
